#  ★2018 부분 표본 제외, 주 2019-24, 보조 2013-17, 전 기간 민감도. 분모 = 대사 위험요인 표 수검자(수검자 조건부 유병). 단독 저자 무인칭, 미국식 철자.

INTRO = [
"Raised blood pressure is a leading modifiable risk factor for cardiovascular disease worldwide [@gbd2025global], and vascular mortality rises continuously with usual blood pressure down to at least 115/75 mm Hg [@lewington2002age]. The number of people with hypertension has grown with population ageing, while treatment has expanded [@ncdrisc2021worldwide]. In the metabolic syndrome, elevated blood pressure is defined as systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or higher, diastolic pressure of 85 mm Hg or higher, or antihypertensive drug treatment [@grundy2005diagnosis; @alberti2009harmonizing]. This criterion therefore counts both people with measured elevation and people whose pressure is lowered by treatment.",
"In Korea, the National Health Insurance Service screens most adults every 2 years and publishes, for every district, how many examinees meet each metabolic syndrome criterion [@kang2022current; @cheolseong2017data; @web:kosis2026screening]. Among Korean examinees, elevated blood pressure is the most common criterion, and its age- and sex-specific prevalence has been described nationally [@jang2026age]. Hypertension prevalence, treatment, and control differ by socioeconomic position and region in Korea [@lee2017socioeconomic; @lee2019temporal; @kim2026korea], but the national pattern does not show where elevated blood pressure at screening is more or less common than expected from the age and sex of those screened.",
"Area characteristics could shape this geography. Aerobic exercise lowers blood pressure [@cornelissen2013exercise], and people living in more walkable neighborhoods, or moving to them, had a lower incidence of hypertension [@sarkar2018neighbourhood; @chiu2016moving], a pattern also described in a review of the urban built environment [@lai2023associations]; sports facilities per resident are a much coarser indicator of such opportunities. Obesity raises blood pressure [@hall2015obesity], reducing heavy drinking lowers it [@roerecke2017effect], and lower socioeconomic status is associated with hypertension [@leng2015socioeconomic]. Because the screening criterion includes treatment, access to medical care could raise the count of examinees who meet it, unlike measures restricted to untreated or undiagnosed hypertension.",
"This study used 6 years of national screening statistics to describe where elevated blood pressure at screening is more common than expected after standardization for sex and age across all Korean districts, and to examine whether its geography follows physical activity and sports facilities, blood pressure risk behaviors, or screening and care reach. Because the published statistics do not separate measured elevation from treatment, results were compared with 2 related outcomes from the same screening program, examinees already treated for hypertension and examinees newly suspected of hypertension, which do not partition the criterion but indicate whether an association is closer to treated or to untreated hypertension.",
]

METHODS = [
("Study design and data",
 "This ecological panel study covered all {n_districts} districts (si, gun, and gu) of the Republic of Korea from 2019 to 2024 ({n_rows} district-years). The outcome was the annual number of general health screening examinees who met the elevated blood pressure criterion of the metabolic syndrome, as published by district and sex (and nationally by sex and age) in the national health screening statistics: systolic blood pressure of 130 mm Hg or higher, diastolic blood pressure of 85 mm Hg or higher, or current antihypertensive treatment [@web:kosis2026screening; @grundy2005diagnosis]. The denominator was the number of examinees in the same statistics, so the outcome is prevalence conditional on attending screening. Sejong, reported only at the province level, was included as 1 district, and districts were linked by administrative codes. The outcome was selected among 23 candidate district screening and mortality indicators because its standardized ratio showed spatial clustering and a stable ranking.",
 "The published criterion was compared with the published distribution of measured blood pressure before modeling. Across provinces, the share meeting the criterion correlated with the share with systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or higher (r = {gate_r_min:.2f}-{gate_r_max:.2f} by year, excluding 2018), and the ratio of the 2 shares rose from {gate_ratio_2013:.2f} in 2013 to {gate_ratio_2024:.2f} in 2024 while the measured share stayed between {gate_sbp_min:.1f}% and {gate_sbp_max:.1f}%, which is consistent with a growing treated component, although diastolic pressure of 85-89 mm Hg could not be separated in the published distribution and the comparison is not age-standardized (Online Resource 1, Tables S2 and S7). The metabolic syndrome table begins in 2013. In 2018 it counted {exam2018_m:.1f} million examinees, one-third of those with measured blood pressure, for reasons not documented in the published statistics, and the share meeting the criterion among women was {f2018:.1f}% compared with about 36% in adjacent years; because comparability with other years was uncertain, 2018 was excluded, the main analysis used 2019-2024, 2013-2017 was analyzed separately, and 2013-2024 including 2018 was a sensitivity analysis.",
 "Expected counts were obtained by indirect standardization [@inskip1983methods]. National rates of the criterion among examinees in 2 sexes and 15 age groups (19 years or younger, 13 five-year groups from 20-24 to 80-84 years, and 85 years or older) were applied to the examinee age structure of each district, approximated from the resident population by sex and age [@web:mois2026population] and national age-specific screening rates and rescaled to the observed number of examinees of each sex, because district examinees by age are not published; standardization is therefore approximate. Expected counts were rescaled each year so that national expected and observed totals matched; the estimand is therefore the district's risk relative to the nation in the same year. In a sensitivity analysis, expected counts were based on the resident population; this changes the estimand to prevalence that also reflects who attends screening."),
("District characteristics",
 "Ten characteristics were examined in 3 groups, with directions set before modeling (Online Resource 1, Table S1). Physical activity was represented by the age-standardized walking and moderate-or-higher physical activity rates from the Community Health Survey [@kang2015korea] and by public and village sports facilities per 100,000 residents (expected lower counts). Blood pressure risk behaviors were represented by the survey's self-reported obesity, high-risk drinking, and current smoking rates (expected higher counts). Screening and care reach were represented by screening uptake (attendees as a share of those invited; expected lower counts through dilution by healthier attendees), internal medicine specialists per 100,000 residents (expected higher counts because the criterion includes treatment), and the public assistance recipient rate, a composite marker of deprivation and of Medical Aid coverage (expected higher counts). All models included 5 fixed adjustments: urban population share, physicians per 1,000 residents, sex ratio, fiscal autonomy, and the share aged 80 years or older. Obesity was not surveyed in 2019 and was replaced by the district mean of the other years (229 of 1,374 district-years), and implausibly low public assistance values in 2021-2023 in 10 cities with general districts (30 district-years) were set to missing and interpolated. Hit-and-run crashes, a traffic culture index, and fires served as negative-control exposures [@lipsitch2010negative]. The covariate sets and model rules were fixed and their files hashed before any model with these characteristics was fitted."),
]
